{
  "summary": "Restricts the -bytespersigop configuration setting at node startup to the range [0, 21474] to prevent integer overflow in mempool sigop-adjusted virtual size calculations. In addition, moves the mempool standard sigops cost check earlier in PreChecks so transactions are verified against the limit before being staged in the changeset. Adds functional test coverage for the parameter boundary and package sizing in mempool_sigoplimit.py.",
  "problem": "-bytespersigop is parsed as an int64_t and stored as an unsigned int, allowing negative numbers to wrap and large numbers to be truncated or overflow int32_t mempool size metrics. Such overflows cause debug builds to crash on Assume assertions in fee rate and transaction graph calculations. Furthermore, checking MAX_STANDARD_TX_SIGOPS_COST only after staging allows transactions with excessive sigops to trigger overflow crashes even under valid -bytespersigop configurations.",
  "evidence": [
    "Commit fac9c100c7 explains that above 21474, a standard 25-transaction package overflows INT32_MAX (25 * 4000 * 21475 = 2147500000) and aborts debug builds on Assume(virtual_bytes >= 0) in CFeeRate::GetFee, while values above 134217 abort single transactions on Assume(feerate.size > 0) in TxGraphImpl::AddTransaction.",
    "Commit 7d37a758b3 shows that staging occurs before the sigop cost limit check: an otherwise standard transaction with two P2WSH inputs totaling 100,040 sigops results in 100040 * 21474 = 2148258960, which exceeds INT32_MAX and triggers an assertion abort during staging."
  ],
